What gets generated: for each tool, the LLM writes realistic user requests and the exact arguments. The arguments are checked against the tool's schema, and any that don't match are dropped. Code then builds the questions, so the targets are exact:

Question	Type
Which tool fits this request? (right one plus wrong ones)	Choice
Which value for a fixed-value argument? Which number (1, 5, 10, 20, 50, 100 plus the default and bounds)?	Choice
Is <tool> the right tool? (asked once for the right tool, once for a wrong one)	Yes/no
Is an optional argument given? Is a true/false argument true?	Yes/no
How completely does the request specify the call? (vague, partial, complete)	Score
Samples: each sample also keeps the full ground-truth call. Samples in the list show it as tool({...arguments}). The new code is in toolgen.rs and SysOneToolsCard.tsx.

Limits to know about:

Free-text arguments: values like an ID or a date copied from the request can't be chosen from a list. They are kept in the stored call but don't become questions yet. Those need the span-extraction question type from my earlier proposal.
Long samples: tool names are the options, so samples are long. I raised the option label limit from 8 to up to 32 tokens, which only matters when an option is long, such as a tool name. Use a max sequence length of 512.
